{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChFNvBmh1KBXAG9svRY1i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All three checklist items for ticket 06 are done. Resume code already existed on the branch, but a run actually interrupted with Ctrl-C could not resume. I fixed that. Build, lint and the full `npm test` pass with no failures.\n\n**The bug.** The loop wrote the ticket review log into the main checkout but only committed it when the loop finished. After an interrupt the log was left as an uncommitted file. The next run then stopped with \"Working tree is dirty\" before it got to the resume check. The existing resume tests missed this because they committed the log themselves before running.\n\n**Changes, one commit each:**\n1. **Refactor.** The fresh path and the resume path each had a full copy of the round loop. I merged them into one function, `runAdversarialLoop` in `src/orchestrator.ts`, which takes a starting round, so the fix only had to go in one place. The review-integration suite passed before and after.\n2. **Fix.** The loop now commits the review log after every entry it writes. I added a test that runs the real CLI: the second reviewer is made to hang, the run gets a SIGINT, and then it is run again. It checks that the interrupted run leaves a clean tree, that the second run exits 0 with the ticket implemented only once, that the ticket is `done` and merged, and that the log shows round 1, round 2 and a pass. Before the fix, this test failed on the dirty-tree error. To make it possible I added a `hang` response to the fake pi.\n3. **Items 2 and 3** (branch head no longer matches the log, and log ends in a final entry). Tests for these already existed, so I checked they really catch a broken resume check: removing each check makes its test fail. I added one assertion to each that the old branch's commits do not end up on main.\n4. **Docs.** The README's `.redeye.json` section now explains resume and that log entries are committed as they are written. AGENTS.md has notes on the per-entry commits, the shared loop function and the fake pi `hang` response.\n\n**Still open:** tickets running at the same time in one wave can both commit to the main checkout at once and hit git's `index.lock`. Progress saving already has the same risk. A failed log commit is logged and the run carries on, but that interrupted ticket would then hit the dirty-tree error again on the next run. My notes are in `.redeye/notes.md`, which is not committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":303,"cache_read_input_tokens":125683,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":303},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37a0e722-681e-439b-ad4f-0778a23ce452","uuid":"b6dc9f81-43a6-4e93-9d60-31fcc3df202b","timestamp":"2026-11-21T03:40:11.886Z","request_id":"req_011ChFNvBdFw6Hyk1nqoP766"}
